</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Full Pipeline Results Across All Models</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ B.3 Full Pipeline Results Across All Models ‣ Appendix B Additional Results ‣ Toward Reliable Clinical Coding with Language Models: Verification and Lightweight Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> presents the complete pipeline results for all models using different expansion and verification methods. When analyzed alongside Table <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.2 Verification Results Across All Models ‣ Appendix B Additional Results ‣ Toward Reliable Clinical Coding with Language Models: Verification and Lightweight Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we can observe that the effectiveness of our verification pipeline depends on both the verification accuracy and the performance of the generation model. Models with stronger generation performance (e.g., Sonnet-3.5v1 and Haiku-3 Finetuned) require higher verification accuracy to achieve meaningful improvements, while weaker models (e.g., Haiku-3 and PLM-ICD) generally benefit from verification.</p>
</div>
